t have seen  a stronger defense against the taliban  could we not have seen it much harder  for the taliban to take over  stop  we did not put a corrupt government in  place corruption has existed in  afghanistan for a long time and here's a  news flash under the taliban corruption  will still exist  the u.s interest in the interest of nato  as a whole  was to prevent a terrorist takeover we  didn't have to reform afghanistan it was  probably a mistake to try  but the real question when you say but  all these things i'd like to just jump  in there because it is a line mr bolton  there's a line we hear how much risk we  hear a lot  we hear a lot  from the us that afghanistan was rife  with corruption but the us did nothing  to stop that corruption i'd just like to  i'd just like to talk about the last  speaker of the afghan parliament he's a  multi-millionaire and that is thanks to  monopoly contracts to provide fuel and  security to u.s forces at bagram the  main  air base the u.s certainly did nothing  to stop corruption  well that's simply not true but let me  ask you a question  uh and i wish you would let me finish so  that we could have this discussion how  much risk are you prepared to accept for  a terrorist attack on london  i mr bolton you know full well that i am  not in command of a country of any of  any sort this is not a question for me  to answer this is a question for our  political leaders to answer and this is  not how the interview works look by your  own measures of success which you have  claimed to be to stop the taliban to  stop al-qaeda from regaining a  capability to have a privileged  sanctuary from which they could plan and  direct attacks on countries like the u.s  by those very metrics the mission in  afghanistan failed i just would like to  know again how was it allowed to fail  it failed because of the decisions by  trump and biden to withdraw  for the last seven or eight years  uh we were muddling along quite well  without terrorist attacks the measure of  success is not whether afghanistan has  been turned into the switzerland of  central asia the issue is whether we are  protecting our civilian populations and  since the tragedy of september the 11th  there have been no terrorist attacks on  the united states conducted out of  afghanistan by al-qaeda or anything else  i call that a success  is the war on terror over  of course not as i indicated before  because of this uh  catastrophic decision to withdraw  we now face i would say the likelihood  of a return of the terrorist threat  uh and and that's why all of these  measures all these things you're talking  about about the corruption of afghan  society and and the faults of the afghan  people i'm very sorry for the afghan  people but let's be clear the united  states and nato did not go into  afghanistan to conduct charity uh for  the people there they've gained a lot of  collateral benefits  from our presence and and i'm glad that  they got them but we're there to protect  our owners as innocent civilians and we  have succeeded in doing that up until  this withdrawal  okay um i'll put it to another way there  may be  cambodia vietnam  the kurds in syria and now afghanistan  why is it that the u.s is such a bad  ally  well i think uh  that you know you're completely  mischaracterizing very very different  circumstances how did germany and japan  feel about america as an ally  these are  issues that you have to take under their  own circumstances and i'd be happy to  take them for as long as you want to  talk but to run up a list like that  as if that's a complete list of what  american policy it consisted of is a  total distortion of reality  you will admit though that it would be  hard for other potential allies to see  themselves i mean we keep hearing from  uh interpreters in afghanistan who say i  would not let my children uh work with  the us if they came back into  afghanistan because we know what that  means now but you'll work with them and  you'll be abandoned it would be easy to  see why future allies might not want to  work with the us um i just i because we  are running out of time  or is that you you know you said you  know what no okay you can  you can you absolutely can we're short  on time but please do well thank you  that's proves the point that we should  not have withdrawn  it is an enormous strategic setback for  the u.s what we've seen uh happening the  scenes we've seen unfolding uh over the  last couple of weeks in kabul um you  could say i mean i hate to draw  comparisons i know you don't like them  but it is as big almost a humiliation as  as vietnam was for the usa how  emboldened do you think and you touched  on this at the beginning of the  interview will the us says strategic  competitors i'm thinking of china i'm  thinking of russia how emboldened will  they be by what they've seen  well i think it's a mistake because it  says to our adversaries that the biden  administration is weak  uh and i don't think that's where the  american people are there are polls that  show just this weekend that when when  asked if if you knew that american  withdrawal would lead to taliban coming  back into power if american withdrawal  would lead to attacks from al qaeda  would you still favor withdrawal and the  answer is no  so this is a mistake in policy uh it's  it's always a tragedy when america makes  a mistake like this  but it does not i think  uh  determine inevitably what the future  will be that's why we have elections in  this country  john bolton uh former u.s national  security advisor thank you so much for  being with us here tonight on

